# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843> (<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:19366698") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843> "Phospholipid N-methyltransferase (PLMT) enzymes catalyze the S-adenosylmethionine-dependent methylation of ethanolamine-containing phospholipids to produce phosphatidylcholine. Phosphatidylcholine is the most abundant phospholipid in most non-plastid membranes of eukaryotes and functions as a structural membrane lipid, and as a precursor to important lipid-based signaling molecules. In mammals and fungi, phosphatidylcholine can be formed through two distinct pathways: (a) the “nucleotide pathway” in which free choline is incorporated in phosphatidylcholine using CDP-choline as an intermediate, and (b) the “methylation pathway” whereby phosphatidylcholine is produced directly from phosphatidylethanolamine (PtdEtn) via three sequential methylation reactions using S-adenosylmethionine (AdoMet) as the methyl donor. In plants, phosphatidylcholine biosynthesis occurs through a branched pathway that utilizes components of both the nucleotide and methylation pathways. In mammals, the 18-kDa integral membrane protein phosphatidylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PEMT) is a PLMT that is expressed primarily in the liver. PEMT catalyzes all three of the methylation reactions needed to convert PtdEtn to phosphatidylcholine.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasOBONamespace> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843> "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#id> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843> "HMDM:0000843")
SubClassOf(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000843> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012>)

# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000844> (<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000844>)

