SubClassOf(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000844> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000015>)

# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845> (<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:27142107") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845> "Analgesics are the drugs which bring insensibility to pain without loosing consciousness. Treatment strategy is generally based on the type of pain. Most of the analgesics are associated with serious side effects, such as NSAIDS can cause severe GI disturbance and opioids can cause addiction.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasOBONamespace> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845> "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#id> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845> "HMDM:0000845")
SubClassOf(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073>)

# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846> (<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846>)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> "PMID:26823273") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846> "Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP) is a cofactor for about 4% of all biochemical reactions. PLP and five other ‘vitamers’, are collectively known as vitamin B6. In general, there are two independent de novo pathways that generate PLP: the deoxyxylulose 5′-phosphate (DXP)-dependent pathway, which is present in most eubacteria (e.g. Escherichia coli), and the ‘DXP-independent’ route, which is widespread (e.g. some eubacteria, Archaea, fungi and plants) and consists of a single enzyme complex, PLP synthase. In the PLP salvage pathway, however, different vitamers are interconverted using various kinase, phosphatase and pyridoxine/pyridoxamine 5′-phosphate oxidase (PNPOx) enzymes. The reaction catalysed by members of the PNPOx family (so-called PdxH proteins) is equivalent to the last step of the DXP-dependent PLP biosynthesis pathway, oxidizing either PNP or PMP to produce PLP. Members of the family utilize flavin mononucleotide (FMN) as a prosthetic group, oxidizing the C4′ alcohol (PNP) or amine (PMP) group to an aldehyde (PLP). During catalysis, FMN is used as the immediate electron acceptor and molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor, forming hydrogen peroxide under aerobic conditions. Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate biosynthesis is essential for the survival and virulence of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb), the organism that causes tuberculosis (TB) and is, for this reason, an important drug target at a time when resistance is rising and new drugs are urgently needed.")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasOBONamespace> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846> "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism")
AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#id> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846> "HMDM:0000846")
SubClassOf(<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012>)

# Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000847> (<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000847>)

